UTIME(2)                       P32W Unix                        UTIME(2)

NAME
       utime - set file times

SYNOPSIS
       #include <utime.h>

       int utime(const char *path, const struct utimbuf *times);

              struct utimbuf { time_t actime, modtime; };

DESCRIPTION
       utime sets the modification time of the file path to
       times->modtime, in seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC. If
       times is a null pointer, it sets it to now. The change time
       becomes now either way.

       The file's owner and the super-user may set any time; with a
       null times, anyone who may write the file may set it to now.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EPERM   The caller is not the owner or the super-user (and,
               for a null times, may not write the file).
       ENOENT  path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path.
       EFAULT  path or times is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       stat(2), time(2), touch(1)

BUGS
       actime is ignored: the filesystem keeps no access time.
       Where POSIX says EACCES for a null times, P32W says EPERM.
